TI  - Decidua-accumulated effector-like memory CD8+ T cells orchestrate localized 
      antimicrobial defense without compromising fetal tolerance.
LID - e202874 [pii]
LID - 10.1172/JCI202874 [doi]
AB  - Deciphering immune privilege at the maternal-fetal interface is essential for 
      understanding how reproductive success and host defense are simultaneously 
      maintained. Decidualization, occurring within the specialized stromal tissue 
      encapsulating the fetus and placenta, shapes local immune adaptation required for 
      healthy pregnancy. Here, we identify a decidua-enriched effector-like memory CD8+ 
      T cell subset marked by CD45RA in humans and CX3CR1 in mice. These cells 
      preferentially accumulate in the decidua, which harbors a CX3CL1-dominant 
      chemokine niche, and disruption of Cx3cr1 or uterine Cx3cl1 impaired their 
      decidual recruitment and tissue localization. Human CD45RA+ and murine CX3CR1+ 
      decidual memory CD8+ T cells shared conserved transcriptional and functional 
      features, including enhanced cytotoxic potential and limited inflammatory 
      cytokine production. Functionally, CX3CL1-mediated CD8+ T cell accumulation 
      improved local control of prenatal Listeria monocytogenes infection and reduced 
      infection-associated fetal loss. In contrast, fetal antigen-specific CD8+ T cell 
      accumulation and fetal resorption occurred independently of uterine CX3CL1. 
      Together, our findings define the maternal-fetal interface as an active 
      immunological gatekeeper, that compartmentalizing CD8+ T cell-mediated 
      antimicrobial adaptive immunity through the conserved CX3CL1-CX3CR1 axis, 
      supporting pathogen-specific defense while preserving fetal tolerance.
