TI  - Efficacy of hysteroscopic curettage and niche resection in type II and III 
      cesarean scar pregnancies.
PG  - 2753981
LID - 10.1080/14767058.2026.2753981 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: To evaluate the efficacy and safety of hysteroscopic curettage 
      combined with cesarean scar diverticulum canal-opening after focused ultrasound 
      ablation surgery (FUAS) pretreatment in patients with type II and III cesarean 
      scar pregnancy (CSP). METHODS: Clinical data of 105 patients with type II/III CSP 
      treated between January 2019 and October 2022 were retrospectively analyzed. All 
      patients received FUAS pretreatment. The observation group (n = 47) underwent 
      hysteroscopic curettage combined with niche canal-opening, while the control 
      group (n = 58) received hysteroscopic curettage alone. Surgical parameters, 
      postoperative recovery, complications, reintervention rates, and menstrual 
      outcomes were compared. RESULTS: The observation group had significantly shorter 
      operation time [20 (15,25) vs. 25 (20,31) min, p = 0.001], less intraoperative 
      blood loss [5 (5,10) vs. 10 (10,50) mL, p = 0.002], lower complication rate [4.3% 
      vs. 17.2%, p = 0.038], and lower reintervention rate [4.3% vs. 19.0%, p = 0.023]. 
      No between-group differences were observed in postoperative hemoglobin decline, 
      hospital stay, vaginal bleeding duration, human chorionic gonadotropin 
      normalization time, menstrual recovery time, or residual scar myometrial 
      thickness (all p > 0.05). Among patients with preoperative prolonged 
      menstruation, menstrual duration improved in both groups (p < 0.05), with a 
      higher improvement rate in the observation group [75.0% vs. 23.1%, p = 0.005]. 
      CONCLUSIONS: FUAS pretreatment combined with hysteroscopic surgery is safe and 
      effective for type II/III CSP. Adding diverticulum canal-opening to hysteroscopic 
      curettage further optimized surgical outcomes by reducing operation time, 
      intraoperative blood loss, complication rates, and the need for reintervention, 
      while also significantly improving prolonged menstruation. This combined strategy 
      may serve as an optimized minimally invasive alternative for patients desiring 
      fertility, though long-term reproductive safety requires further verification.
