TI  - Multi-Niche Microbiome Alterations in Endometrial Cancer: Drivers, Biomarkers and 
      Translational Targets.
PG  - 643435
LID - 10.2147/IJWH.S643435 [doi]
LID - 643435
AB  - Endometrial cancer (EC) is the commonest gynecological malignancy in high-income 
      countries and one of the few solid tumors whose incidence and mortality are 
      rising despite advances in oncological care. Conventional hormonal, metabolic and 
      genetic risk factors fail to fully explain EC tumorigenesis, and the multi-niche 
      human microbiome has emerged as a novel, modifiable regulatory axis governing EC 
      onset, progression and immunotherapy responsiveness. However, existing reviews 
      have largely examined individual anatomical sites in isolation, and a 
      cross-compartment synthesis that integrates the gut, cervicovaginal, uterine 
      cavity, and intratumoral niches-together with their mechanistic and therapeutic 
      implications-remains lacking. This narrative review screened English articles 
      from PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus and Google Scholar using keywords covering 
      EC, microbiome, reproductive tract microbiome alterations, the estrobolome and 
      immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs), prioritizing prospective cohorts, clinical 
      trials and multi-omics mechanistic studies. We synthesize evidence across four 
      interconnected niches and integrate them into a unified mechanistic framework. 
      Reproducible compartmental signatures characterize EC: cervicovaginal samples 
      show severe Lactobacillus depletion and enrichment of pathogenic anaerobes 
      including Porphyromonas, Prevotella and Atopobium; malignant endometrial tissues 
      display elevated α-diversity dominated by Anaerococcus, Peptoniphilus and 
      Gardnerella, together with poorly explored fungal community shifts; the 
      intratumoral niche harbors distinct taxa that directly modulate tumor 
      proliferation, immune evasion and therapeutic responsiveness; and gut microbiome 
      alterations deplete short-chain fatty acid-producing Firmicutes while expanding 
      β-glucuronidase-rich Enterobacteriaceae, driving estrogen reactivation via the 
      estrobolome and systemic pro-tumor inflammation. The PRIMMO trial further 
      validates gut microbial clusters as predictive biomarkers of pembrolizumab 
      efficacy in recurrent EC. By positioning the intratumoral microbiome as a 
      distinct niche and linking multi-niche alterations to hormonal, inflammatory, 
      immunological and genotoxic pathways, this review provides a new 
      cross-compartment perspective that reframes the microbiome as both a mechanistic 
      contributor and a translational target in EC. Standardized analytical workflows 
      and large-scale prospective cohorts are urgently needed to translate these 
      discoveries into routine clinical practice.
