TA  - Int J Womens Health
JT  - International journal of women's health
JID - 101531698
PMC - PMC13718827
OAB - WHY DID WE WRITE THIS REVIEW? Endometrial cancer is the most common gynecological 
      cancer worldwide, and its death rate is rising. Traditional risk factors like 
      hormones and obesity do not fully explain why some women develop this disease. 
      The trillions of bacteria living in our bodies—our “microbiome”—may play an 
      important role. WHAT DID WE FIND? ● The uterus is not sterile. Harmful bacteria 
      can be detected in the uterine cavity of women with endometrial cancer, where 
      they may encourage cancer growth.● Protective Lactobacillus bacteria are depleted 
      in the vagina and uterus of these patients, while harmful bacteria like 
      Porphyromonas and Prevotella become more abundant.● Certain gut bacteria 
      reactivate spent estrogens, potentially increasing lifelong estrogen exposure—a 
      key driver of this cancer. WHAT DOES THIS MEAN? These findings open new 
      possibilities. Simple vaginal swabs could one day help screen for endometrial 
      cancer, and adjusting the gut microbiome might improve how patients respond to 
      immunotherapy. While more research is needed, the microbiome offers a promising 
      new approach to understanding, detecting, and treating this increasingly common 
      cancer.
