TI  - Long-term gynecological symptoms and ultrasound and reproductive outcomes after 
      monofilament vs multifilament uterine closure at Cesarean delivery: secondary 
      analysis of randomized controlled trial.
PG  - 714-719
LID - 10.1002/uog.70365 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To evaluate long-term gynecological symptoms and reproductive and 
      ultrasound outcomes among women originally enrolled in a randomized controlled 
      trial (RCT) comparing monofilament and multifilament sutures for uterine closure 
      after Cesarean delivery. METHODS: This was a secondary analysis of a RCT 
      including women who underwent Cesarean delivery, who were originally assigned to 
      monofilament or multifilament absorbable sutures for uterine closure. In this 
      secondary analysis, participants were contacted 4-6 years after delivery and 
      invited to a follow-up evaluation, comprising a clinical interview and a 
      voluntary transvaginal ultrasound examination. The primary outcome was the 
      presence of postmenstrual spotting at long-term follow-up. Secondary outcomes 
      were other gynecological symptoms, reproductive outcomes and ultrasound outcomes. 
      Analyses were performed according to the original randomized allocation. Effect 
      estimates are reported as relative risk (RR) with 95% CI. RESULTS: Of the 300 
      women included in the original RCT, 117 (39.0%) were included in the long-term 
      follow-up analysis, comprising 51 in the monofilament group and 66 in the 
      multifilament group. There was no significant difference in the primary outcome 
      of postmenstrual spotting between the monofilament and multifilament groups 
      (23.5% vs 22.7%; RR, 1.04 (95% CI, 0.53-2.01)). Similarly, no significant 
      differences were observed between groups for other gynecological symptoms. 
      Ultrasound data were available for 21 women, of whom 13 had a Cesarean-scar 
      defect. The overall prevalence of Cesarean-scar defects was similar between 
      groups, although defects ≥ 4 mm were more frequent in the multifilament group 
      (RR, 0.42 (95% CI, 0.18-0.97); P = 0.04). Most reproductive outcomes, including 
      infertility and pregnancy outcomes among women who conceived, were comparable 
      between groups. However, subsequent pregnancy occurred more frequently in the 
      monofilament group (29.4% vs 12.1%; RR, 2.43 (95% CI, 1.12-5.27)). CONCLUSIONS: 
      In this long-term follow-up of a RCT, monofilament and multifilament sutures for 
      uterine closure were associated with similar long-term gynecological symptoms. 
      Differences observed in selected ultrasound and reproductive outcomes should be 
      interpreted cautiously because of the limited sample size and exploratory nature 
      of these analyses. Further studies are needed to better evaluate the potential 
      impact of suture material on long-term reproductive outcomes and sonographic 
      Cesarean-scar characteristics. © 2026 The Author(s). Ultrasound in Obstetrics & 
      Gynecology published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of International Society 
      of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology.
