TI  - Patient-Reported Tolerability, Treatment Initiation, and Discontinuation of 
      Postoperative Progestin Therapy After Cesarean Scar Repair in Women Planning 
      Pregnancy.
LID - 10.3390/medicina62101996 [doi]
LID - 1996
AB  - Background and Objectives: Cesarean scar disorder (CSDi) may require surgical 
      repair in women with a large niche and thin residual myometrium. At our center, 
      temporary postoperative progestin therapy is recommended to suppress cyclic 
      endometrial proliferation and menstrual activity at the freshly reconstructed 
      scar during early wound healing; whether this improves structural healing is 
      unknown. We evaluated patient-reported treatment initiation, discontinuation, 
      tolerability, bleeding patterns, mood-related symptoms, sexual behavior, and 
      previous hormonal treatment experience in women planning pregnancy. Materials and 
      Methods: This retrospective single-center cohort study included 74 women who 
      underwent laparoscopic-assisted cesarean scar repair between 2020 and 2023. 
      Seventy completed questionnaire follow-ups between March and October 2024 
      (response rate 94.6%). Hormonal exposure was patient-reported and included 
      desogestrel, dienogest, or an unspecified progestin. Analyses were primarily 
      descriptive and used item-specific denominators. Results: Among women with valid 
      postoperative treatment information, 43/55 (78.2%) reported progestin use. 
      Very-high motivation or a pregnancy-related motivation response was selected by 
      43/59 women (72.9%). Premature discontinuation because of adverse effects was 
      reported by 15/61 (24.6%), and prolonged vaginal bleeding >14 days by 15/64 
      (23.4%). Mean perceived adverse-effect intensity was 3.43 (SD 2.99) on a 10-point 
      scale. Fatigue, irritability, and depressed mood were the most frequently 
      reported mood-related symptoms. Among women comparing the postoperative regimen 
      with previous hormonal preparations, 20/32 (62.5%) reported no relevant 
      difference in tolerability. Conclusions: Most women with available treatment 
      information initiated postoperative progestin therapy, and motivation remained 
      substantial despite an active desire for pregnancy. A relevant subgroup reported 
      adverse-effect-related discontinuation or prolonged bleeding. These findings 
      support careful pretreatment counseling and shared decision-making. The effects 
      of postoperative hormonal suppression on anatomical healing and reproductive 
      outcomes remain uncertain.
