TI  - Surgery-related factors predisposing to uterine niche after cesarean section: a 
      systematic review.
PG  - 1838075
LID - 10.3389/fmed.2026.1838075 [doi]
LID - 1838075
AB  - Cesarean section is among the most common surgical procedures worldwide, with 
      rates exceeding 48% in some European countries and continuing to rise for both 
      primary and repeat cases. Multiple cesarean deliveries have become routine in 
      many developed countries. This trend has increased the incidence of intrapartum 
      and long-term complications, particularly cesarean scar defects, which are 
      symptomatic in up to 40% of affected women. A cesarean scar defect, also known as 
      a uterine niche or isthmocele, is one of the most critical conditions in modern 
      obstetrics and gynecology, with continuously increasing prevalence, as 56%-84% of 
      women who undergo cesarean section develop a niche. Judicious indication for 
      cesarean delivery and an appropriate uterine suturing technique are key factors 
      in minimizing post-cesarean complications, including uterine niches. Uterine 
      niche epidemiology and risk factors are widely investigated with partially 
      contradictory conclusions. In this systematic review, a detailed analysis of 
      surgery-related factors connected to uterine niche formation is presented. 
      Aspects of uterine suturing technique, including the number of suture layers, 
      locked or unlocked suturing, inclusion of the endometrium, and the choice of 
      suture material, are compared. The aspect of single- and double-layer uterine 
      closure remains under debate, as most studies do not show the superiority of 
      either technique with regard to uterine niche incidence. Optimizing the 
      hysterotomy surgical technique may decrease the rate of cesarean scar defects and 
      their complications, including pelvic pain, abnormal uterine bleeding, 
      dyspareunia, placenta accreta spectrum disorders, and cesarean scar pregnancies. 
      All patients undergoing cesarean delivery should be screened for cesarean scar 
      defects and informed about the potential complications of cesarean section.
