TI  - Case report of a 33-year-old patient with a niche in a cesarean section scar.
PG  - 375-379
LID - 10.36740/Merkur202603118 [doi]
AB  - Worldwide, cesarean section procedures are being performed with increasing 
      frequency, which is associated with a growing number of postoperative 
      complications. One of these is the formation of a triangular, anechoic defect of 
      the uterine myometrium at the site of a previous cesarean section scar, referred 
      to as a niche. This condition may lead to abnormal bleeding, pain, fertility 
      problems, and complications in subsequent pregnancies. This paper presents the 
      case of a 33-year-old patient with a history of two cesarean sections, in whom 
      ultrasonographic examination revealed a defect within the scar measuring 4 mm in 
      depth, consistent with a so-called niche. The patient expressed an intention to 
      conceive again; however, the presence of the defect constituted a significant 
      risk factor for complications during pregnancy. The importance of accurate 
      diagnosis and appropriate management of the niche before a planned pregnancy was 
      emphasized to minimize the risk of serious complications, such as uterine scar 
      dehiscence. This case highlights the need for proper management of women with a 
      cesarean section scar who are planning subsequent pregnancies.
