TI  - Niche presence and changes of the uterine scar in the first year after caesarean 
      section: A prospective longitudinal study.
PG  - 115051
LID - S0301-2115(26)00117-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ejogrb.2026.115051 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To investigate the presence of uterine niches and changes in the 
      uterine scar during the first year after caesarean section and determine the 
      optimal time for scar evaluation. STUDY DESIGN: This prospective study included 
      women who underwent their first caesarean section between May 2019 and November 
      2021. Transvaginal ultrasound was performed at 6 weeks, 10 weeks, 6 months, and 
      12 months post-surgery. Data on uterine niche presence and scar characteristics 
      were systematically collected. RESULTS: Ninety-four women were included; 79 
      (84.0%) had repeated measurements. Attendance declined from n = 73 at 6 weeks to 
      n = 53 at 12 months. At 6 weeks, a niche was observed in 62/73 (84.9%), with 
      62.9% of these classified as large.At 12 months, niche prevalence decreased to 
      41/52 (78.8%), with 53.7% classified as large. The uterine scar characteristics 
      changed over time. Mixed model analyses revealed a significant decrease in RMT 
      between 6 weeks and 6 months (-1.1 mm, 95% CI: -1.9 to -0.2, p = 0.02), followed 
      by an increase between 6 and 12 months (+1.0 mm, 95% CI: 0.1 to 1.7, p = 0.04). 
      Furthermore, significant differences in scar characteristics over time were 
      found; length, depth and width decreased up to 6 months. CONCLUSION: Uterine scar 
      measurements and niche prevalence decreased up to 6 months post-caesarean 
      section, with a subsequent increase in RMT up to 12 months. It is therefore 
      recommended to postpone evaluation of the uterine scar until at least 6 months 
      after caesarean section, as most changes occur within this initial period.
