TI  - Sonographic Assessment of Isthmocele and Its Obstetric Complications in 
      Subsequent Pregnancies: A Pictorial Review.
PG  - 159-168
LID - 10.1055/s-0044-1788588 [doi]
AB  - Cesarean scar defect represents a significant pathology attributed to the rising 
      prevalence of cesarean deliveries. While not commonplace, these lesions can give 
      rise to severe obstetric consequences during subsequent pregnancies. Given the 
      potential complications, it is advisable to screen for uterine niches using 
      transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) or contrast-enhanced TVUS for individuals planning 
      to conceive. Surgical repair and correction of these lesions can be crucial in 
      averting obstetric and perinatal complications in future pregnancies. 
      Furthermore, timely sonographic evaluation and reporting of isthmocele-related 
      obstetric complications can help avoid serious issues.
