DP  - 2023 Jun
TI  - Mucinous Cystadenoma Arising in a Uterine Isthmocele: A Case Report.
PG  - 333-336
LID - 10.1055/s-0043-1770090 [doi]
AB  - Isthmocele is a discontinuation of the myometrium at the uterine scar site in a 
      patient with a previous cesarian section (CS). The cause of isthmocele appears to 
      be multifactorial. Poor surgical technique, low incision location, uterine 
      retroflection, obesity, smoking, inadequate healing of scars, and maternal age 
      are possible related factors. Most patients with this condition are asymptomatic. 
      However, women can present with postmenstrual bleeding, pelvic pain, 
      subfertility, dysmenorrhea, infertility, and scar abscess. Brazil has one of the 
      world's highest cesarean section rates. One of the consequences of the rising 
      rate of CS is the isthmocele, an emerging female health problem. Here we report a 
      case of mucinous cystadenoma arising in a uterine isthmocele, a complication, as 
      far as we could investigate, not yet described in the literature.
