TI  - When and how should we treat cesarean scar defect - isthmocoele?
PG  - 664-668
LID - 10.5603/GP.2016.0063 [doi]
AB  - The reported number of cesarean sections in Poland is approximately 30% and is 
      associated with increasing number of early and late complications. The myometrial 
      discontinuity at the site of previous cesarean section is known in the literature 
      as "isthmocoele", "niche", "pouch" or cesarean scar defect. In most cases 
      presence of isthmocoele has no clinical significance, but in some patients it may 
      cause abnormal uterine bleeding, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, pelvic pain or be 
      associated with secondary infertility. This defect may be treated by laparoscopy, 
      hysteroscopy or vaginal surgery.
