TI  - The sterile and tolerogenic fetal niche does not restrict the generation of CD4 T 
      memory cells.
PG  - 64
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2305-5839.2015.02.02 [doi]
LID - 64
AB  - T-cell activation requires a sequence of signals derived from co-stimulatory 
      receptors and from immunogens that may or may not be of infectious origin. This 
      scenario provides the threshold of inflammatory stimulus needed for the induction 
      of antigen-specific T cell responses. One of the dogmas of immunology stipulates 
      that the activation of T lymphocytes is prevented in immunosuppressed or 
      tolerogenic environments. However, it was shown recently that a healthy uterine 
      environment that is considered sterile, therefore not exposed to infection, is 
      capable of generating T memory cells with the capacity to differentiate 
      lineage-specific inflammatory effector T-cell responses. The implications of this 
      finding are discussed in this editorial.
